 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on",
   "text": "GM2 AB variant happens when a child has changes in a gene called GM2A instead of it working normally [E2252927, Edcf2ca7, E38e152d, Ec4c4b25, E8577cd5, E4676f6b, E35d8336, E2c5bd51]. This gene is supposed to make a small helper protein that works with another protein to break down a fatty substance in cells, called GM2 ganglioside [E330669b]. When the GM2A gene does not work, cells cannot clear away this fatty substance, so it builds up inside nerve cells [Eb15acdf, E503f243, E7d4ef12, Ed2a63e3]. A paper reports that the faulty helper protein also gets broken down too early, before it can leave the cell's processing area, which is called the endoplasmic reticulum [E91b9ab5]. According to HPO, children with this condition can have stiff or weak arms and legs, very strong startle reactions, extra sensitivity to sound, low energy or interest, and jerky movements [E99a6889, E8b10733, E9795eb1, E7132f60, E922ff7f, Edcea2c5]. According to ClinVar, one specific change in the GM2A gene, called c.188del, has been found to cause GM2 AB variant [Ebe484ca].",
