  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "Diseases with similar biology",
   "text": "Tay-Sachs disease shares the same core problem as GM2 AB variant: a paper reports that in Tay-Sachs the body also cannot break down GM2 ganglioside, so it builds up in nerve cells [E0d0eb92, E7c4296e, Ee7edb35]. Both conditions sit in the same body pathway for handling fat-like molecules, called glycosphingolipid metabolism, according to Reactome [Ee27a382, E41520eb, E1656f87]. According to HPO, children with GM2 AB variant and Tay-Sachs can both have apathy and trouble controlling their head [E922ff7f, Ecc9b1b9, E98a460a, E11599d9]. Krabbe disease, late-onset Parkinson's, Gaucher disease, and Fabry disease also sit in that same glycosphingolipid pathway, even though each is caused by a different gene, according to Reactome [Ee27a382, E6be1459, E8a2d502, Eaeb2dd9]. According to HPO, Krabbe disease and GM2 AB variant can both involve stiff muscles and loss of nerve cells over time [E3b6cdca, Ebb770bf, Ef793a01, E861bd0e, E6b7d4d8, E0936c1c].",
